W1 (terminal) and J3 (honor). So no.\n\nLooking for possible discards. The valid actions are to play one of the listed tiles. The key is to discard a tile that is least needed for forming a winning hand.\n\nLooking at the tiles, which ones are critical? The T8 has two copies, which could form a pair. If I discard a T8, I can have a single T8, which might not be helpful. Alternatively, if I keep both T8s as a pair, that could be part of the hand.\n\nOther tiles: W1 is a terminal. If I discard W1, maybe it's not critical. But if I need to form a chow that includes W1, but there's no such possibility.\n\nLooking at the Dots: B2, B4, B8, B9. B8 and B9 are higher numbers. If there's a B7, could form 7-8-9, but I don't have B7. B2 and B4 could be part of a chow if there's a B3, but I don't have B3. So maybe B2 and B4 are not useful. Discarding one of them might not affect the hand.\n\nHonors: J3 is a single tile. If I discard it, but there's no way to form a pung. So maybe it's better to discard J3 if it's not part of any pattern.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard B2, which is part of the Dots, maybe that's a better choice. Let's see.\n\nIf I discard J3, then the hand becomes 12 tiles, and I need to draw one more. But the melds are still empty. The hand would have W1, W3, W4, W7, T2, T3, T4, T8, T8, B2, B4, B8, B9. Then if I draw a tile, maybe a T8 to complete a pair, but already have two T8s. Or maybe another tile to form a chow.\n\nAlternatively, discarding B2: the hand would have W1, W3, W4, W7, T2, T3, T4, T8, T8, B4, B8, B9, J3. Then if I draw a tile, maybe a T1 to form a chow with T2-T3, but I don't have T1. Or maybe a T5 to form a chow with T3-T4-T5, but I don't have T5. So not helpful.\n\nDiscarding T3: the T2 and T4 would remain. If I discard T3, then T2 and T4 are left. Maybe forming a chow of T2-T4 and something else? No, chows need consecutive numbers. So discarding T3 would break the T2-T3-T4 chow. That's bad. So better not to discard T3.\n\nDiscarding T4: same 