orming a pair. But which is better?\n\nIf I discard B8, I have one B8 left. If I discard W7, I have W8 left. Let's see which gives more flexibility. If I discard B8, I can still form a pair with the remaining B8, and maybe a Chow with B7 and B8 and B9. If I discard W7, I can form a pair with W8, but need another W8. The unshown has four W8s, so it's possible. But which is more likely to lead to a winning hand?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard a tile that's not part of any meld. For example, W7 or W8. Since I have two of them (W7 and W8), but only one each. If I discard one, I can focus on forming a pair with the remaining one. For example, if I discard W7, I have W8 left. If I draw another W8, I can form a pair. Then my hand would have two Chows (B and T), a pair of W8, and maybe another meld. The remaining tiles would be W4, B7, and maybe others. If I can form a Chow with W4 and others, but I don't have W2 or W5. So maybe not. Alternatively, if I can form a Pung of B7 with another B7, but I have only one.\n\nHmm. This is tricky. Maybe the best option is to discard B8, keeping the pair and the potential for the B7-B8-B9 Chow. That way, I have two Chows (B and T), a potential third Chow (B7-B8-B9), and a pair. If I can get B9, that's three melds and a pair, needing one more. The remaining tiles would be W3, W4, W7, W8. Maybe a pair of W7 and W8? But they are different numbers. Not a pair. Unless I can form a Chow with them and another tile. For example, W7-W8-W9. But I don't have W9. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W7, I have W8 left. If I get another W8, that's a pair. Then the remaining tiles are W3, W4, B7. Maybe a Pung of B7 with another B7. But again, only one in hand.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any current meld and that has more copies left in the wall, reducing the risk of being blocked. For example, W7 has 3 copies left, W8 has 4. So discarding W7 might be safer if I need to